2. Right-click Databases > Create > Database. Name it dataengineering (or the exact database name your instructor requires).
3. Create a table named users. The manual's screenshot does not provide a fully readable textual schema. Later practicals refer to userid, name, age, street, city, state and zip; Practical 2's Airflow code selects name and city. Coordinate the actual table columns with the schema in your lab.
4. One compatible example schema for the later people-data stages is shown below. Treat this as a setup template if the existing table does not already exist:

