10. Create spark_dataframe.py and paste the complete program below. It corresponds to the operations listed on pages 95-99 of the manual.

CODE - spark_dataframe.py
from pyspark.sql import SparkSession
import pyspark.sql.functions as F

spark = (
    SparkSession.builder
    .appName("Spark-DataFrame-Processing")
    .master("local[*]")
    .getOrCreate()
)

print("Spark Version:", spark.version)
df = spark.read.csv("data.csv", header=True, inferSchema=True)

print("\n1. COMPLETE DATASET")
df.show(truncate=False)

print("\n2. DATAFRAME SCHEMA")
df.printSchema()

print("\n3. SELECT NAME AND AGE")
df.select("name", "age").show()

print("\n4. PEOPLE UNDER 40")
df.filter(F.col("age") < 40).show()

print("\n5. PEOPLE OVER 40")
df.filter(F.col("age") > 40).show()

print("\n6. PEOPLE FROM MAHARASHTRA")
df.filter(F.col("state") == "Maharashtra").select(
    "name", "age", "city", "state"
).show()

print("\n7. PEOPLE SORTED BY AGE")
df.orderBy("age").show()

print("\n8. AGE STATISTICS")
df.describe("age").show()

print("\n9. PEOPLE COUNT BY STATE")
df.groupBy("state").count().orderBy("state").show()

print("\n10. AVERAGE AGE")
df.agg(F.avg("age").alias("average_age")).show()

print("\n11. MINIMUM AND MAXIMUM AGE")
df.agg(
    F.min("age").alias("minimum_age"),
    F.max("age").alias("maximum_age")
).show()

print("\n12. DISTINCT STATE COUNT")
df.select(F.countDistinct("state").alias("distinct_states")).show()

df.createOrReplaceTempView("people")
print("\n13. SQL QUERY: PEOPLE AGED OVER 30")
result = spark.sql("""
    SELECT name, age, city, state
    FROM people
    WHERE age > 30
    ORDER BY age DESC
""")
result.show()

print("\n14. ADD AGE CATEGORY")
df_with_category = df.withColumn(
    "age_category",
    F.when(F.col("age") < 30, "Young")
     .when(F.col("age") < 45, "Adult")
     .otherwise("Senior")
)
df_with_category.select("name", "age", "age_category").show()

print("\n15. TOTAL RECORDS")
print("Total Records:", df.count())
spark.stop()
print("\nSpark practical completed successfully.")

11. Execute: spark-submit spark_dataframe.py from the folder containing data.csv.
12. Check that each output heading appears, filters produce the expected subset, grouped counts are produced, and the final message appears.

Code explanation / key terms
- SparkSession.builder creates/configures the Spark application session.
- appName gives the application a readable name.
- master("local[*]") asks Spark to use all local logical CPU cores; this is local mode, not a multi-machine cluster.
- read.csv loads a CSV file. header=True treats the first row as column names; inferSchema=True asks Spark to infer types.
- show prints rows; printSchema displays column names and types; count returns row count.
- select chooses columns; filter keeps rows satisfying a condition; orderBy sorts; describe provides summary statistics.
- groupBy(...).count() counts records per group; avg/min/max/countDistinct are aggregate functions.
- createOrReplaceTempView makes a temporary SQL view; spark.sql runs SQL against that view.
- withColumn adds/replaces a column; when/otherwise create the age_category rule.
- spark.stop releases the Spark session.

Expected output
A set of tables and aggregates for the dataset. Exact names, averages, groups and row counts depend on the exact data.csv values copied from the manual.

